<div style="background-color: #1a5276; color: white; padding: 15px; text-align: center; border-radius: 8px;">
    <h1 style="margin: 0;">Lesson Planner – Theme # ___ – Lesson # 5</h1>
    <p style="margin: 5px 0 0 0;"><em>Complete this planner five times per theme, once per lesson (Lesson 1 through Lesson 5).</em></p>
</div>

<table style="border: 1px dashed #333; border-collapse: collapse; width: 100%; margin: 10px 0;">
    <tr>
        <td style="border: 1px dashed #333; padding: 8px; width: 25%;"><b>Lesson #:</b> 5</td>
        <td style="border: 1px dashed #333; padding: 8px; width: 75%;"><b>Skills Focus:</b> Mediation</td>
    </tr>
    <tr>
        <td style="border: 1px dashed #333; padding: 8px;"><b>Grade:</b> 2nd Grade (CEFR: Pre-A1.4)</td>
        <td style="border: 1px dashed #333; padding: 8px;"><b>Scenario:</b> All About the Weather</td>
        <td style="border: 1px dashed #333; padding: 8px;"><b>Theme:</b> Dress for the Weather.</td>
    </tr>
    <tr>
        <td style="border: 1px dashed #333; padding: 8px;"><b>Date(s):</b> From ___ to ___</td>
        <td style="border: 1px dashed #333; padding: 8px;" colspan="2"><b>Learning Sequence Time:</b> 45–50 min</td>
    </tr>
    <tr>
        <td style="border: 1px dashed #333; padding: 8px;" colspan="3">
            <p style="text-align: justify; margin: 4px 0 8px 0;"><b>Specific Objective:</b> Mediation: Text: Use visual information in texts to convey meaning. Communication: Describe everyday objects using visual support.</p>
            <p style="text-align: justify; margin: 4px 0 8px 0;"><b>Learning Outcome:</b> Mediation: Can use visual information in texts by pointing to related images during stories (e.g., points to clouds when discussing "It's cloudy."). Can describe everyday objects using visual support (e.g., points to the sun in a weather chart and says "sunny"). Can participate in weather chants by pointing to visuals (e.g., points to an umbrella during a "rainy day" song).</p>
        </td>
    </tr>
</table>

<h2 style="color: #1a5276;">The Six Action-Oriented Approach (AOA) Lesson Stages</h2>
<table style="border: 1px dashed #333; border-collapse: collapse; width: 100%; margin: 10px 0;">
    <thead>
        <tr>
            <th style="border: 1px dashed #333; padding: 8px; background-color: #f2f2f2; width: 30%;">Stage & Description</th>
            <th style="border: 1px dashed #333; padding: 8px; background-color: #f2f2f2; width: 55%;">Activities & Teacher Actions</th>
            <th style="border: 1px dashed #333; padding: 8px; background-color: #f2f2f2; width: 15%;">Estimated Date and Time</th>
        </tr>
    </thead>
    <tbody>
        <tr>
            <td style="border: 1px dashed #333; padding: 8px;">
                <p style="text-align: justify; margin: 4px 0 8px 0;"><b style="color: #1a5276;">STAGE 1 — Warm-up / Pre-task</b></p>
                <p style="text-align: justify; margin: 4px 0 8px 0;">Engagement, Modeling and Clarification</p>
            </td>
            <td style="border: 1px dashed #333; padding: 8px;">
                <p style="text-align: justify; margin: 4px 0 8px 0;"><b>Warm-up:</b> The teacher holds up large flashcards showing different weather conditions (sunny, rainy, cloudy, windy) and corresponding clothing items (hat, umbrella, coat, jacket). Teacher points to a weather card and asks, "What's the weather like?" Students respond chorally "It's [weather]!" Then the teacher shows a clothing item and asks, "What do I wear?" Students respond "A [clothing item]!" This activates prior knowledge and connects to the theme "Dress for the Weather." </p>
                <p style="text-align: justify; margin: 4px 0 8px 0;"><b>Modeling:</b> The teacher models describing the weather and suggesting clothing using the visuals. For example, the teacher points to the "sunny" flashcard and says, "It's a sunny day! I wear a hat." Then points to the "rainy" flashcard and says, "It's a rainy day. I need my umbrella." The teacher explicitly uses gestures to mediate the meaning of the words and shows how to connect the visual weather to the visual clothing. Pronunciation of /s/ in "sunny" and /r/ in "rainy" is emphasized.</p>
                <p style="text-align: justify; margin: 4px 0 8px 0;"><b>Clarification (CCQs):</b> The teacher checks understanding using visual cues and simple questions.
                    <ul style="list-style-type: disc; padding-left: 20px; margin-top: 0; margin-bottom: 0;">
                        <li style="margin-bottom: 4px; text-align: justify;">(Holding up 'sunny' card, pointing to 'coat') "Do I wear a coat for a sunny day?" (Expected answer: No!)</li>
                        <li style="margin-bottom: 4px; text-align: justify;">(Holding up 'rainy' card, pointing to 'umbrella') "Is this an umbrella for rainy weather?" (Expected answer: Yes!)</li>
                    </ul>
                </p>
            </td>
            <td style="border: 1px dashed #333; padding: 8px;">10 min</td>
        </tr>
        <tr>
            <td style="border: 1px dashed #333; padding: 8px;">
                <p style="text-align: justify; margin: 4px 0 8px 0;"><b style="color: #1a5276;">STAGE 2 — Presentation</b></p>
                <p style="text-align: justify; margin: 4px 0 8px 0;">Input of new language/content connected to Scenario: "All About the Weather".</p>
            </td>
            <td style="border: 1px dashed #333; padding: 8px;">
                <p style="text-align: justify; margin: 4px 0 8px 0;"><b>Material:</b> A simple, illustrated "Weather Forecast" chart or a short comic strip with 3-4 panels depicting different weather conditions (e.g., sunny, rainy, windy) and appropriate clothing. Each panel has a simple sentence like "Today is sunny. I wear my hat." or "Tomorrow is rainy. I need my umbrella." </p>
                <p style="text-align: justify; margin: 4px 0 8px 0;">The teacher displays the visual weather forecast and reads each panel aloud, pointing to the pictures as they speak. Students listen and follow along, pointing to the corresponding visuals in their individual copies or on the board. The teacher models the pragmatic function of "Describing the weather and activities: 'It's a sunny day, and I play outside.'" and "Responding to questions: 'What's the weather like?' 'It's rainy. I need my umbrella.'"</p>
                <p style="text-align: justify; margin: 4px 0 8px 0;"><b>Communicative Competence:</b> Describing the weather and activities: "It's a sunny day, and I play outside." Responding to questions: "What's the weather like?" "It's rainy. I need my umbrella."</p>
                <p style="text-align: justify; margin: 4px 0 8px 0;"><b>Comprehension tasks:</b>
                    <ul style="list-style-type: disc; padding-left: 20px; margin-top: 0; margin-bottom: 0;">
                        <li style="margin-bottom: 4px; text-align: justify;"><b>Gist questions:</b> The teacher asks, "What's the weather like today?" (Students point to the sunny panel). "What's the weather like tomorrow?" (Students point to the rainy panel).</li>
                        <li style="margin-bottom: 4px; text-align: justify;"><b>Matching:</b> The teacher says a word (e.g., "windy," "cloudy," "boots"), and students point to the corresponding image on the weather forecast chart or comic strip.</li>
                    </ul>
                </p>
            </td>
            <td style="border: 1px dashed #333; padding: 8px;">10 min</td>
        </tr>
        <tr>
            <td style="border: 1px dashed #333; padding: 8px;">
                <p style="text-align: justify; margin: 4px 0 8px 0;"><b style="color: #1a5276;">STAGE 3 — Preparation / Practice</b></p>
                <p style="text-align: justify; margin: 4px 0 8px 0;">Accuracy focus on Mediation.</p>
            </td>
            <td style="border: 1px dashed #333; padding: 8px;">
                <p style="text-align: justify; margin: 4px 0 8px 0;"><b>Communicative Skill & Content:</b> Students will practice using visual information to convey meaning and describe objects. Grammar: Adjectives + nouns ("rainy day"), Present simple ("I like sunny days."), WH-questions ("What is the weather like?"). Vocabulary: sun, rain, cloud, wind, umbrella, coat, hat, boots, sunny, rainy, cloudy, windy, wear, like. Pronunciation: /s/ in "sunny", /r/ in "rainy", initial /w/ and final /i/ in "windy." Sociolinguistic: Expressing preferences ("I like sunny days.").</p>
                <p style="text-align: justify; margin: 4px 0 8px 0;"><b>Detailed Procedure:</b>
                    <ul style="list-style-type: decimal; padding-left: 20px; margin-top: 0; margin-bottom: 0;">
                        <li style="margin-bottom: 4px; text-align: justify;"><b>1. Controlled practice – "Picture Match & Say":</b> The teacher distributes worksheets with various weather pictures and a word bank of clothing items (e.g., hat, umbrella, coat, boots). Students draw a line to match each weather picture (e.g., 'sunny day') to the correct clothing item (e.g., 'hat'). Then, students point to each pair and complete a simple sentence orally, chorally, or individually: "It's a [sunny] day. I wear a [hat]." This activity reinforces mediation by connecting visuals to words and simple sentences.</li>
                        <li style="margin-bottom: 4px; text-align: justify;"><b>2. Semi-controlled practice – "Weather & Wear Wheel":</b> Students use a pre-made "Weather and Wear" wheel. The wheel has two layers: an inner wheel with weather pictures (sunny, rainy, cloudy, windy) and an outer wheel with clothing pictures (hat, coat, umbrella, scarf). Students spin the wheel to align a weather picture with a clothing picture. They then practice saying, "What's the weather like? It's [cloudy]. I wear a [jacket]." The teacher encourages the use of "I like sunny days" or "I like rainy days" based on the visual. Teacher corrects pronunciation of /s/, /r/, and /w/.</li>
                        <li style="margin-bottom: 4px; text-align: justify;"><b>3. Pair interaction/peer check – "Forecast Buddy":</b> Students work in pairs. Each pair has a set of weather flashcards and clothing flashcards. Student A picks a weather flashcard, shows it to Student B, and asks, "What's the weather like?" Student B points to the weather card, describes it ("It's [rainy]."), and then selects an appropriate clothing flashcard, saying, "I need my [umbrella]." Student A checks if the clothing choice is correct based on the weather. They then switch roles. This fosters mediation by having students interpret and convey visual information to their peer.</li>
                    </ul>
                </p>
            </td>
            <td style="border: 1px dashed #333; padding: 8px;">15 min</td>
        </tr>
        <tr>
            <td style="border: 1px dashed #333; padding: 8px;">
                <p style="text-align: justify; margin: 4px 0 8px 0;"><b style="color: #1a5276;">STAGE 4 — Performance / Production</b></p>
                <p style="text-align: justify; margin: 4px 0 8px 0;">Communicative task where students USE the language in a realistic scenario: "All About the Weather".</p>
            </td>
            <td style="border: 1px dashed #333; padding: 8px;">
                <p style="text-align: justify; margin: 4px 0 8px 0;">Students will work in small groups (2-3 students) to present a mini "Weather Forecast Report" based on their 21st Century Skills Project (Weather Forecast Video / Comic Strip). They will use their drawn comic strips or storyboards as visual aids for their report.</p>
                <p style="text-align: justify; margin: 4px 0 8px 0;"><b>Task Format:</b> Small group presentation/demonstration. Each group presents their comic strip/storyboard to the class. Students act as "weather reporters," taking turns pointing to different panels/drawings in their comic strip and describing the weather and appropriate clothing. They will use phrases like: "Hello! Today it's a [sunny] day. I like sunny days. I wear a [hat]." or "Look, tomorrow is [rainy]. I need my [umbrella]." One student might ask, "What is the weather like?" and another points to a picture and answers, "It's [windy]!" They are mediating the visual information from their project into spoken language.</p>
                <p style="text-align: justify; margin: 4px 0 8px 0;"><b>Connection to Theme:</b> "Dress for the Weather." The project directly involves predicting weather and choosing clothing. Students will be explaining their choices using visual support.</p>
                <p style="text-align: justify; margin: 4px 0 8px 0;"><b>Focus:</b> Fluency in simple sentences, collaborative interaction within the group, and clear communication of the weather forecast using visual aids.</p>
            </td>
            <td style="border: 1px dashed #333; padding: 8px;">10 min</td>
        </tr>
        <tr>
            <td style="border: 1px dashed #333; padding: 8px;">
                <p style="text-align: justify; margin: 4px 0 8px 0;"><b style="color: #1a5276;">STAGE 5 — Assessment / Post-task</b></p>
                <p style="text-align: justify; margin: 4px 0 8px 0;">Formative assessment strategy.</p>
            </td>
            <td style="border: 1px dashed #333; padding: 8px;">
                <p style="text-align: justify; margin: 4px 0 8px 0;"><b>Formative Assessment Strategy:</b> Teacher observation checklist, focused on each student's ability to use visuals to convey meaning.</p>
                <p style="text-align: justify; margin: 4px 0 8px 0;"><b>Assessment Task:</b> During the group "Weather Forecast Report" presentations, the teacher uses an observation checklist to assess individual student performance. Each student is expected to point to at least one weather visual (from their comic strip/storyboard) and describe it with a simple sentence (e.g., "It's sunny. I wear a hat.")</p>
                <p style="text-align: justify; margin: 4px 0 8px 0;"><b>Evaluation Criteria:</b>
                    <ul style="list-style-type: disc; padding-left: 20px; margin-top: 0; margin-bottom: 0;">
                        <li style="margin-bottom: 4px; text-align: justify;"><b>Intelligible pronunciation:</b> Pronounces /s/ in "sunny", /r/ in "rainy", and initial /w/ and final /i/ in "windy" clearly.</li>
                        <li style="margin-bottom: 4px; text-align: justify;"><b>Use of target vocabulary:</b> Employs words like "sunny," "rainy," "cloudy," "windy," "hat," "coat," "umbrella," "boots."</li>
                        <li style="margin-bottom: 4px; text-align: justify;"><b>Use of target grammar:</b> Correctly uses "It's a [adjective] day" and "I wear/need a [noun]." (Adjectives + nouns, Present simple).</li>
                        <li style="margin-bottom: 4px; text-align: justify;"><b>Clarity and mediation:</b> Clearly uses visual support to convey the meaning of their weather report. Their pointing aligns with their words.</li>
                        <li style="margin-bottom: 4px; text-align: justify;"><b>Collaboration:</b> Participates actively and takes turns in the group presentation.</li>
                    </ul>
                </p>
                <p style="text-align: justify; margin: 4px 0 8px 0;"><b>Student Self-Assessment / Exit Ticket:</b> Students complete a simple exit ticket by checking boxes or drawing happy/sad faces.
                    <ul style="list-style-type: disc; padding-left: 20px; margin-top: 0; margin-bottom: 0;">
                        <li style="margin-bottom: 4px; text-align: justify;">I can show the weather with a picture and say "It's [weather]." (Yes/No/A little)</li>
                        <li style="margin-bottom: 4px; text-align: justify;">I can tell what clothes to wear for the weather. (Yes/No/A little)</li>
                    </ul>
                </p>
            </td>
            <td style="border: 1px dashed #333; padding: 8px;">10 min</td>
        </tr>
        <tr>
            <td style="border: 1px dashed #333; padding: 8px;">
                <p style="text-align: justify; margin: 4px 0 8px 0;"><b style="color: #1a5276;">STAGE 6 — Reflection</b></p>
            </td>
            <td style="border: 1px dashed #333; padding: 8px;">
                <p style="text-align: justify; margin: 4px 0 8px 0;"><b>Student self-reflection prompt:</b>
                    <ul style="list-style-type: disc; padding-left: 20px; margin-top: 0; margin-bottom: 0;">
                        <li style="margin-bottom: 4px; text-align: justify;">What did I like most about telling the weather story with my pictures today?</li>
                        <li style="margin-bottom: 4px; text-align: justify;">What was a little hard for me when I showed my weather report?</li>
                    </ul>
                </p>
                <p style="text-align: justify; margin: 4px 0 8px 0;"><b>Teacher reflection:</b>
                    <ul style="list-style-type: disc; padding-left: 20px; margin-top: 0; margin-bottom: 0;">
                        <li style="margin-bottom: 4px; text-align: justify;">What worked well in this mediation lesson? The visual support (comic strips, flashcards) was very effective in helping students connect spoken words to images. Students enjoyed presenting their "weather reports" with their drawings. The focus on pointing and describing enhanced their mediation skills.</li>
                        <li style="margin-bottom: 4px; text-align: justify;">What adjustments and scaffolds are needed? Some students needed more explicit modeling for the full sentence structure "It's a [weather] day. I wear a [clothing item]." More choral repetition of these complete sentences could be beneficial. Future lessons could incorporate more complex visual texts.</li>
                    </ul>
                </p>
                <p style="text-align: justify; margin: 4px 0 8px 0;"><b>Connection to next lesson in the theme sequence:</b> This lesson concludes the "Dress for the Weather" theme. The next thematic unit will continue to build on visual literacy and mediation skills by introducing new vocabulary and scenarios, always integrating visual aids and opportunities for students to connect images to spoken language.</p>
            </td>
            <td style="border: 1px dashed #333; padding: 8px;">5 min</td>
        </tr>
    </tbody>
</table>